mantle rm236
state ready
dialect plain
accent light broad
tier standard
written_for rm_ansel_wickdale
title the skyline carver
fits stage=adult|old
prefers job=laborer
traits temper=even talk=quiet warmth=reserved humour=none honesty=plain piety=ordinary drive=content
quirk cuts_mast_profiles_into_scrap_wood
likes oatcakes whittling fresh_straw thunder
dislikes salt_wind drizzle
fears losing_a_familiar_outline
hopes to_fit_a_harbour_in_a_drawer
topics ships +0.5 pastimes +0.4 land +0.2 gossip -0.4
styles comfort=quiet gossip=withhold refuse=soft tease=none
marks oath saying
persona #self.first# pares scrap wood into the outlines of distant ships, a patient observer with small, exact answers.

words
oath! = Tar on the thumbnail | Splinter catch it
saying! = Shave away the sky | Leave the grain its sea room
scrap = a scrap of pale wood | a slip of dry deal
cutout = a narrow wooden skyline | my little cutout
slot = the nick for a mast | the slot at the stern
hull = the dark undercurve | the long belly of a boat
chine = the turn of the side | the lower bend
sky = the light behind it | the bright strip behind the hull
blade = my little paring blade | the small worn cutter
chips = the pale rubbish | a scatter of curls
thumb = my measuring thumb | a thumbnail at arm's length
bag = the pocket for offcuts | my bag of useful ends
straw = a fresh straw twist | a pale stem
supper = an oat round | my flat little cake

any
opinion city for 1 : From low down even an ordinary roof makes a grand line against the sky.
opinion houses against 2 : I'd be glad if some rent collectors would come down and count the loose steps too.
opinion dole for 2 : There's precious little carving to be done with a belly biting itself. Feed folk first.
opinion merchants against 1 : The curl of wood costs nothing and the finished thing costs dear. Somebody's paws are paying for the difference.
opinion church for 1 : There's room on a chapel bench for a quiet wolf with empty pockets.
opinion watch against 1 : A patrol sees a row of faces. I wonder how many faces see a helping paw.

grown
line greet : Good to see a shape I recognise. | Move over a shaving's width. There's space.
line greet [pair.band=strangers] : Hello there. I'm making nothing urgent. | Mind this curl underfoot. Bit slippery.
line greet.return : Quite clear from where I'm sitting. | I heard. Welcome alongside.
line greet.return [self.working=yes] : One slip, then I'm listening. | You caught me mid-cut. Nearly there.
line greet.return [pair.band=friends|family] : I'd know that outline anywhere. | Your shadow fell just right for recognition.
line greet.return [here.time=night] : Dark round the edges, but I know that voice. | Evening to your shadow too.
line again.open : Our two outlines match up again. | Another glimpse of the familiar.
line kin.open [pair.kin!=no] : Come close. You can look over my shoulder. | I kept a smooth bit for family.
line farewell : Keep the light behind the thing you're looking for. | I'll clear the chips after you go.
line farewell.return : Straight walking and no splinters. | I'll mark the last cut in our conversation.
line react.news : Which part could you see from there? | Give me the outline first.
line react.news.new : I had no shape for that in mind. | That's a cut I'd never have guessed at.
line react.news.known : I know that silhouette well enough. | I've heard the edges of that account already.
line react.news.doubt : There's a piece missing from that outline. | Let me see it from one other angle first.
line react.news.sorrow [topic.kind=death|loss|theft|assault|arrest|crime] : Hard news to mark on wood. | I'll lay my work aside to hear you.
line react.news.glad [topic.kind=marriage|birth|apprentice|succession|work] : A pleasing turn there. Worth remembering. | That'll keep its shape a long while.
line ask.how : Has your day gone with the grain? | How's the view from where you stand?
line answer.how : Quietly pleased with a small thing. | Busy fingers and a quiet head.
line decline : I'd rather finish the curve before the light changes. | Another occasion might suit the work better.
line accept_decline : Fair. Leave it rough this time. | I'll find a use for the spare space.
line thank : You spared me a nasty split there. | Much appreciated. That edge will keep now.
line deflect_thanks : It only wanted a gentle touch. | Save the praise till it fits.
line preclose : I've the last sliver to take off. | I'll turn back to this scrap before it splits.
line accept_preclose : Leave me with the shavings. Go easy. | I'll see your outline on another day.
line sympathise : I won't ask you to put a shape on it yet. | I'll sit and take none of the quiet.
line ack : The bend, yes. | Just so. A finer line.
scene pier_cut
when topic=pastimes|ships
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I've cut away half the sky. Now you can see a stern. | Hold #cutout# against the window and tilt it.
b : Why cut the sky away? | Is that a vessel turning?
a : Because it's the part of a ship the wood can spare. | It will be once I pare the nose down a shade.

scene thumb_scale
when topic=land|travel
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : My thumb covers a ship if I stand far enough off. | I measure tall things by a thumbnail held sideways.
b : How can that help you? | Always the same thumb?
a : Gives me the size of the scrap I need. | I tried changing paws. My whole harbour listed.

scene pocket_sound
when topic=smalltalk
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : Two wooden hulls in one pocket make a very quiet collision. | I put felt between the finished boats before I walk.
b : Damaged at sea? | You mind the paint?
a : Only by a vigorous walk across a landing. | I leave them plain. It's their delicate ends I'm saving.

scene wick
when topic=work self.working=yes self.job=laborer
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I like laying things out by thickness before carrying them. | A fine stem folded under a heavy coil comes out crooked.
b : Is it quicker your way? | Can you straighten it?
a : Slower to begin and much quicker to put away. | Mostly. A better use of time is packing it high.

scene straw_bed
when topic=home self.likes=fresh_straw
does tell.plan / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I'll bed a little hull down on #straw#. | Good straw earns its keep at both ends of a trip.
b : Instead of floating it? | How's that?
a : It's made to sit in a drawer and remember floating things. | Packing there, a clean floor covering at the far end.

scene oats
when topic=food self.likes=oatcakes
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : One bite from an oat round makes a crescent shore. | I break my oatcake above a dish these days.
b : Going to carve that as well? | Saving the crumbs?
a : Not if my appetite gets there first. | Saving the hulls. Crumbs get into the little nicks for rigging.

scene storm_roof
when topic=weather here.weather=storm
does tell.feeling / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : Thunder leaves me perfectly content to be under a sound roof. | Each peal rattles the drawer in a different key.
b : Don't you jump? | Is there music in it?
a : First one, yes. After that it's fine company. | More of a working harbour all grumbling together.

scene drizzle
when topic=weather here.weather=rain
does tell.complaint / react.complaint / assess
a : Fine rain rubs the outline away before I can set it down. | In this wet every boat looks cut from the same grey slat.
b : Then invent one. | No difference left?
a : I'll wait. An invention never surprises me by turning broadside. | A deeper grey for the nearest. That's about my tool kit today.

scene grain
when topic=pastimes
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail / assess
a : The grain bent before I ever took a blade to it. | This curve already knew what it was to become.
b : You just follow it? | And what if you wanted it straight?
a : Far as it'll put up with company, yes. | Then I'd have picked a much less interesting scrap.
b : That's a courteous way of cutting. | I see why you've so many crooked ships.

scene offcut
when topic=smalltalk
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : Give me a waste end and I'm supplied for an afternoon. | My bag has a separate corner for plain ugly wood.
b : Satisfied with very little. | Saving it for uglier boats?
a : Not little. Look how much mast is hiding in that pointed bit. | For testing a cut before trying it on a handsome one.

scene recognise
when topic=ships|memories
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : Take away the colour and a ship can still be familiar. | A name's easy enough to forget. A mast set too far back stays put.
b : What gives it away? | Wouldn't the name be tidier?
a : The rise between the two ends, even if the rigging's down. | Certainly. But the shape would have to be wrong to fit in a name.

scene honesty
when topic=gossip
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : A story without a clear edge stays out of my pocket. | My rule about a private matter is to leave it uncarved.
b : Unclear how? | In case somebody finds it?
a : Where seeing ends and guessing begins. | In case I start thinking it's mine to display.

scene home_quarter
when topic=home self.quarter=sump
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : Down my way a landing serves as a workshop if you tuck both elbows in. | Sump stairs take an offcut up and bring the shavings back down.
b : People can get by? | Sweeping them down?
a : Sideways. I pick my elbows up on hearing a footfall. | In my own sack. Nobody asked for a drift outside their door.

scene friend
when topic=friends self.friend
does tell.feeling / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I'd lend #self.friend# a finished boat without needing a pledge. | If #self.friend# wanted the best one, I'd struggle to refuse.
b : Even the one you keep by you? | What would be hard about giving it?
a : Most of all that one. It belongs where I'd miss it. | Choosing, I mean. I think highly of several.

scene narrow_days
when topic=times world.times=hard|lean
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : When money's scant a strip of waste timber makes a handsome evening. | Lean days leave a wolf looking hard at the sweepings.
b : I should think firewood comes first. | Anything useful?
a : It does. That's why my boats are so small. | Light for the hearth and a hull besides if luck holds.

scene first_look
when topic=newcomer
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I'd show a newcomer the city's shape from a landing first. | Better go up a little before trying to find your road.
b : Why the height? | To see over the smoke?
a : You can remember a roof even when the alleys tangle. | Over the first row of doors at least. A modest view's useful.

scene salt_air
when topic=land|travel
does tell.feeling / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : The bay's got a fine outline. I'd take the smell from further off. | My lips know when the sea wind's paid a visit.
b : Tired of the salt? | Can you taste the harbour?
a : Yes. It salts an oatcake before I get a say. | Enough to wish I'd wrapped supper better.

scene quiet_prayer
when topic=faith
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I take a plain scrap to chapel in my pocket sometimes. | A clean bit of wood makes a fair resting place for worry.
b : Do you carve on the bench? | How do you leave it?
a : No. I hold it still, which takes a different sort of patience. | Hold it and pray till I can let go.

scene empty_drawer
when topic=dreams|home
does tell.plan / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I'd like a drawer deep enough for a whole Sound of little hulls. | One day I'll arrange the cutouts by the height of their masts.
b : Wouldn't you rather have a shelf? | Tall ones in front?
a : Dust is a terrible tide on a shelf. | Only if the low ones feel bashful.

story chip_fleet [topic=memories|pastimes]
  : My first little vessel snapped clean across the middle. I was about to burn both ends when I noticed they sat quite level.
  : I turned one round, laid both under the sill and got two small boats instead.
  b : Did they look alike?
  : One sailed off with all the cabins. Its partner had rather too many bows.

story shadow_reversed [topic=memories]
  : I cut a hull from the shadow cast on a piece of scrap. Come daylight, the bow pointed where I remembered the stern.
  : I carried it round a post, grumbling at the shape. Then I saw the vessel from the other side.
  b : There was your carving after all.
  : Exactly. I've walked round things before taking offence ever since.

story crumbs [topic=food|pastimes]
  : I once packed my supper in a nest of chips and a new cutout in a napkin. In a poor light I unwrapped the wrong one.
  : There was my supper sailing out of sight into the chips while the cutout settled nicely on the plate.
  b : Tough pastry?
  : Beautiful crust. Needed a lifetime's soaking.

rule @curl
when concept=bark self.likes=whittling
does aside
cooldown 9h
say Save that curled end. There's a stern in it. | Small shavings wanted for a very small fleet.

rule @packing
when concept=bark self.working=yes self.job=laborer
does aside
cooldown 8h
say Leave the light twists above the coil. | Fine end up so it won't crease.

rule @straw
when concept=bark self.likes=fresh_straw
does aside
cooldown 8h
say Straight straw for packing, broken for sweeping! | Lay those pale stems out to air.

rule @view
when concept=bark here.region=ridgemere
does aside
cooldown 12h
say That's a bold line against the Sound. | Room for another mast on my scrap.

dlg resident.hub : Take up a corner of this light with me. I'll put the blade down.
dlg resident.hub [topic.meeting=known] : #topic.player#, you'd recognise this shape. Turn it over.
dlg resident.hub [here.time=night] : Better sit still. Loose chips on a dark floor can catch one out.
dlg resident.hub [pair.disposition=hostile|wary] : Keep a paw's distance from the little carvings. Go ahead.
dlg resident.job [self.job=laborer self.workplace] : #self.workplace# keeps my paws occupied. Fine things need carrying too.
dlg resident.job : I work quietly enough to see each change of outline.
dlg resident.news [world.news] : #world.news#. There's the news without my guesses added.
dlg resident.bye : I'll put the spare bit by for you.
graft resident.hub
choice Why are the ships all flat on one side? -> flat_hulls
node flat_hulls
say They sit against the back of a drawer. Look at the face with the light behind it and all the depth you need comes back.
mood calm
free
choice Can you tell two ships apart like that? -> mast_spacing
choice Let's put the drawer back. -> hub
node mast_spacing
say One's got a little waist between the masts, another a nose like an upturned spoon. I'd know them in a row of kitchen knives.
choice I'll look at the outlines. -> hub
choice Good luck with the fleet. -> bye
